@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.1883 = private unnamed_addr constant [1272 x i8] c"## What it does\0AIdentifies FastAPI routes that declare path parameters in the route path\0Athat are not included in the function signature.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0APath parameters are used to extract values from the URL path.\0A\0AIf a path parameter is declared in the route path but not in the function\0Asignature, it will not be accessible in the function body, which is likely\0Aa mistake.\0A\0AIf a path parameter is declared in the route path, but as a positional-only\0Aargument in the function signature, it will also not be accessible in the\0Afunction body, as FastAPI will not inject the parameter.\0A\0A## Known problems\0AIf the path parameter is _not_ a valid Python identifier (e.g., `user-id`, as\0Aopposed to `user_id`), FastAPI will normalize it. However, this rule simply\0Aignores such path parameters, as FastAPI's normalization behavior is undocumented.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Afrom fastapi import FastAPI\0A\0Aapp = FastAPI()\0A\0A\0A@app.get(\22/things/{thing_id}\22)\0Aasync def read_thing(query: str): ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Afrom fastapi import FastAPI\0A\0Aapp = FastAPI()\0A\0A\0A@app.get(\22/things/{thing_id}\22)\0Aasync def read_thing(thing_id: int, query: str): ...\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as modifying a function signature can\0Achange the behavior of the code.\0A", align 1
