@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.1962 = private unnamed_addr constant [1103 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for executable `.py` files that do not have a shebang.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python, a shebang (also known as a hashbang) is the first line of a\0Ascript, which specifies the interpreter that should be used to run the\0Ascript.\0A\0AIf a `.py` file is executable, but does not have a shebang, it may be run\0Awith the wrong interpreter, or fail to run at all.\0A\0AIf the file is meant to be executable, add a shebang, as in:\0A```python\0A#!/usr/bin/env python\0A```\0A\0AOtherwise, remove the executable bit from the file\0A(e.g., `chmod -x __main__.py` or `git update-index --chmod=-x __main__.py`).\0A\0AA file is considered executable if it has the executable bit set (i.e., its\0Apermissions mode intersects with `0o111`). As such, _this rule is only\0Aavailable on Unix-like systems_, and is not enforced on Windows or WSL.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Executable Python Scripts](https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/appendix.html#executable-python-scripts)\0A- [Git documentation: `git update-index --chmod`](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-update-index#Documentation/git-update-index.txt---chmod-x)\0A", align 1
