@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.2159 = private unnamed_addr constant [1334 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `pathlib.Path.with_suffix()` calls where\0Athe given suffix does not have a leading dot\0Aor the given suffix is a single dot `\22.\22`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`Path.with_suffix()` will raise an error at runtime\0Aif the given suffix is not prefixed with a dot\0Aor it is a single dot `\22.\22`.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Apath.with_suffix(\22py\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Apath.with_suffix(\22.py\22)\0A```\0A\0A## Known problems\0AThis rule is likely to have false negatives, as Ruff can only emit the\0Alint if it can say for sure that a binding refers to a `Path` object at\0Aruntime. Due to type inference limitations, Ruff is currently only\0Aconfident about this if it can see that the binding originates from a\0Afunction parameter annotated with `Path` or from a direct assignment to a\0A`Path()` constructor call.\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThe fix for this rule adds a leading period to the string passed\0Ato the `with_suffix()` call. This fix is marked as unsafe, as it\0Achanges runtime behaviour: the call would previously always have\0Araised an exception, but no longer will.\0A\0AMoreover, it's impossible to determine if this is the correct fix\0Afor a given situation (it's possible that the string was correct\0Abut was being passed to the wrong method entirely, for example).\0A\0ANo fix is offered if the suffix `\22.\22` is given, since the intent is unclear.\0A", align 1
