@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.2262 = private unnamed_addr constant [796 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `raise` statements within `try` blocks. The only `raise`s\0Acaught are those that throw exceptions caught by the `try` statement itself.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ARaising and catching exceptions within the same `try` block is redundant,\0Aas the code can be refactored to avoid the `try` block entirely.\0A\0AAlternatively, the `raise` can be moved within an inner function, making\0Athe exception reusable across multiple call sites.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adef bar():\0A    pass\0A\0A\0Adef foo():\0A    try:\0A        a = bar()\0A        if not a:\0A            raise ValueError\0A    except ValueError:\0A        raise\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef bar():\0A    raise ValueError\0A\0A\0Adef foo():\0A    try:\0A        a = bar()  # refactored `bar` to raise `ValueError`\0A    except ValueError:\0A        raise\0A```\0A", align 1
